Build a premium, minimal, responsive messaging interface.
Required screens:
•	Account creation.
•	Phone-number OTP login.
•	Profile setup.
•	Username management.
•	Contact discovery.
•	Conversation list.
•	Messaging interface.
•	Voice-message recording.
•	File transfer.
•	Incoming calls.
•	Active calls.
•	Call history.
•	Offline status.
•	Transport selection or status.
•	Synchronization status.
•	Storage management.
•	Privacy settings.
•	Device management.
Show accurate connection information, such as:
•	Online.
•	Offline.
•	Local connection available.
•	Internet connection available.
•	Recipient unreachable.
•	Message queued.
•	Transferring.
•	Delivered.
•	Synchronization pending.
Do not show fake online indicators or fabricated delivery receipts.
Use restrained animations and optimize for low-end Android phones and unreliable connectivity.
12. BANGLADESH-FIRST DESIGN
Optimize for actual usage conditions in Bangladesh.
Priorities:
•	Low mobile-data consumption.
•	Small application footprint.
•	Low memory usage.
•	Efficient battery usage.
•	Fast startup on low-end devices.
•	Resilience to unstable connections.
•	Efficient text synchronization.
•	Direct local transfer where supported.
•	Bangla and English localization.
•	International phone-number handling.
•	Support for affordable Android devices.
•	Clear offline operation.
•	Accessible interfaces for ordinary users.
Do not assume every user has a high-end smartphone or continuous broadband connectivity.
Design the application to degrade gracefully when bandwidth, device performance, or network availability is limited.
13. REAL DEVICE TESTING
Build a genuine test plan for offline operation.
Test with at least two independently installed devices.
Required scenarios:
1.	Both devices online.
2.	Sender online and recipient offline.
3.	Sender offline and recipient online.
4.	Both devices offline but connected through a supported local transport.
5.	Message delivery over a direct local connection.
6.	File transfer over a direct local connection.
7.	Voice-message transfer.
8.	Audio calling over an implemented local transport.
9.	Reconnection and synchronization.
10.	Duplicate-delivery prevention.
11.	Interrupted transfer recovery.
12.	Device restart with pending messages.
13.	Contact blocking.
14.	Unauthorized peer attempts.
For offline tests, disable conventional internet access and verify that the intended communication path still works.
Record the exact devices, operating-system versions, transport, and results.
Do not classify a simulation or unit test as a successful real-world offline communication test.
14. DEVELOPMENT EXECUTION
Begin by inspecting the existing repository and development environment.
Then implement the system incrementally.
Phase 1: Architecture and data models.
Phase 2: Supabase backend and authentication.
Phase 3: IndexedDB persistence and synchronization.
Phase 4: Real online messaging.
Phase 5: Contact discovery and identity management.
Phase 6: Voice messages and file sharing.
Phase 7: Internet audio calls.
Phase 8: Offline transport abstraction.
Phase 9: Native Android offline communication.
Phase 10: Native iOS offline communication.
Phase 11: Real-device testing and reliability improvements.
Phase 12: Production deployment and documentation.
Do not stop after producing a plan.
Implement working code, run tests, fix errors, and continue through the available development workflow.
If a feature requires physical hardware or external infrastructure that is unavailable, implement the available components and state exactly what still needs validation.
Do not substitute mock functionality.
15. FINAL ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA
The project is not complete merely because it builds successfully.
It must have:
•	Real authentication.
•	Real contact management.
•	Real messaging.
•	Real local persistence.
•	Real synchronization.
•	Real file transfers.
•	Real voice messages.
•	Real internet audio calls.
•	A defined and implemented offline transport.
•	Accurate delivery acknowledgments.
•	Duplicate prevention.
•	Security policies.
•	Automated tests.
•	A real-device offline validation plan.
•	Documentation for web, Android, and iOS.
Clearly distinguish implemented features from proposed or untested features.
Build the strongest genuinely functional system supported by the available environment.

